        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <br>Currently the impact load on crewmembers landing in the Soyuz vehicle is unknown. This study is the first systematic assessment of the number and types of injuries associated with Soyuz landing. Recent investigations have found that more than a third of US Orbital Segment (USOS) crewmembers are experiencing injuries. Most injuries are minor but exceed expected rates based on analysis of seat accelerometer data from airborne and drop tests of the vehicle. This experiment seeks to tease out the following possible explanations for the prevalence of landing injuries to be higher than expected: (1) spaceflight deconditioning may render crewmembers more susceptible to landing impact injuries, (2) the Soyuz landing load may be higher than current estimates, (3) current analytical tools may be insufficient to accurately predict injury rates for space vehicles, (4) some combination of these factors are responsible.<br><br>This experiment will address four specific aims.<br>1.	Collect retrospective post-landing questionnaire data and develop injury database.<br>2.	Determine the occurrence of landing injuries to crewmembers.<br>3.	Determine whether the Soyuz meets current Multi-Purpose Crew Vehicle (MPCV) and Commercial Crew Program (CCP) requirements.<br>4.	Evaluate whether injury rates are consistent with the results of Finite Element (FE) modeling.<br><ol>
</ol>
<p>
APPROACH: <br>To address Aims 1 and 2, an accurate estimation of the occurrences of injury during Soyuz landings will be determined using data contained in NASA flight medicine databases and supplemented with data collected from crewmembers, flight surgeons, Russian and international partner sources. In addition, post-landing questionnaires administered within one month of landing will be used to supplement the above sources.</p><p>To address Aim 3, Soyuz information will be gathered from Russian colleagues to determine vehicle dynamics during landing. Data from an accelerometer attached to the Soyuz-TMA seat will describe the impact environment crewmembers are exposed to during landing. These data will allow the researchers to obtain actual landing accelerations for individual landings; however, if these data are not available, information about nominal and off-nominal landings will be collected to develop a statistical model of possible landing distributions.</p><p>In Aim 4, a FE model of the Soyuz seat will be developed and the test device for human occupant restraint with modification kit (THOR), Hybrid III anthropometric test device (ATD), and human FE models will be fitted into the seat model. Using the Soyuz dynamics data, either obtained or calculated, computational landing simulations will be then conducted.</p><p>
RESULTS: <br>The results of this experiment will produce several deliverables, including a model assessment of the risk of injury due to Soyuz landing for a variety of landing accelerations, evaluation of the residual risk associated with current MPCV and CCP design requirements, and a Soyuz post-landing database. This information will provide evidence of the risk of injury due to dynamic loads and will contribute to gap closure by characterizing and quantifying the risk associated with the Soyuz landing system.<end></end></p>
        </div>

